<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Krishna Kumari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1794–<a href="/years/1810">1810</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> Kumari was a princess of Mewar, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana">Maharana</a> Bhim Singh. Her hand was sought by the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a>, and their rivalry over the betrothal led to a ruinous war in which the Pindari leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> Khan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> armies also intervened. In 1810, under pressure from Amir Khan, she was given poison to end the dispute. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-tod">James Tod</a>'s account made her story one of the best-known tragedies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> history.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Krishna Kumari was one of several daughters of Bhim Singh, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> ruler of the princely state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> in the Mewar region. In 1799, at the age of five, she was engaged to Bhim Singh, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> in the Marwar region, but he died in 1803. His cousin Man Singh succeeded him and revoked an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> that his predecessor had granted to Kishan Singh, a relative of Krishna's father, which annoyed him. Krishna's father then arranged her engagement to Jagat Singh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, an adversary of Man Singh. Man Singh argued that she was betrothed to the ruler of Jodhpur and should marry him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Man Singh threatened to invade Mewar and enlisted Daulat Rao Sindhia of Gwalior by paying a tribute. Sindhia later invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> and forced Bhim Singh to sign a treaty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nathdwara">Nathdwara</a> on 7 May 1806. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yashwant-rao-holkar">Yashwant Rao Holkar</a> of Indore offered to arbitrate but also wanted tribute. Jagat Singh marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> and besieged it in May 1807, but his army withdrew because of a shortage of water and defections, including Sarjerao Ghatge and Amir Khan. Sindhia then invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> and extracted a heavy tribute, and also attacked Udaipur.</p><p>Amir Khan Pindari, who had changed sides to Man Singh for money, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> in April 1810 and burned villages while its nobles quarrelled among themselves. He demanded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> be married to Man Singh or killed, since there would be no peace while she lived. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> was watching the conflict; in 1806 an official had expected a war that would favour the Company.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A contemporary British account in the Asiatic Annual Register says that Amir Khan suggested poison as the only way to end the pretensions of the suitors. Nobles of Mewar are believed to have felt that the marriage would dishonour the house. Bhim Singh decided his daughter's death was necessary for peace, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> agreed. She died of poison on 21 July 1810, administered, the register says, by her aunt with the father's knowledge. Afterwards Amir Khan, the Chundawat chiefs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> fought for control of Mewar, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> became a British protectorate in January 1818.</p><p>Her fate became known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> through Sir John Malcolm's Memoir of Central India in 1824 and Amir Khan's own memoirs, translated in 1832. It inspired poems by Letitia Elizabeth Landon and others, and prose versions in British magazines.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Krishna Kumari (princess)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…and later the Scindia and Holkar houses exacted heavy tribute, nobles feuded among themselves and the treasury was exhausted; in 1810 the young princess <b>Krishna Kumari</b> was poisoned to avert a war between rival suitors from Jaipur and Jodhpur, an episode that came to symbolise the state’s helplessness.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
